  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n- Rain through Thursday night\n\nA fairly long duration light rain event is unfolding across Lower\nMI. Radar shows light rain from the central UP southwest to New\nMexico along a cold front moving eastward across northern \nWisconsin. This frontal boundary will sag south across the central\ncwa today and then remain quasi stationary until Thursday night. \nMeanwhile, abundant moisture moving north from the Gulf in \nconjunction with subtropical moisture from the Pacific will help \nto create light to moderate rainfall during the next 48 hours. \nWe'll wait until low pressure over the southern Plains finally \ngets kicked out by a northern stream short wave for the frontal \nboundary across the cwa to finally move south. That will allow \ncooler air to move into the region.\n\nInstability is somewhat anemic with this system and so we are\nexpecting mostly rain. However, instability increases a bit\ntonight and we could see a non severe thunderstorm or two south \nof I-96. Total QPF 1-2 inches is possible...highest over the west\ncentral cwa. \n\nRain will end from north to south early Friday once the cold front\nmoves south, leading to some sunshine late in the day.",
